Set `aks_log_retention_days` on the **cluster** to keep AKS control-plane logs. Use a whole number of days from 30 to 730.

{ "aks_log_retention_days": 90 }

The template then creates a Log Analytics workspace with that retention. It turns on the AKS monitoring add-on (Container Insights) and sends the `kube-apiserver` and `kube-audit-admin` logs to the workspace. Container logs use the same retention. The database and cache settings below also use this workspace when it exists.

Set `azure_cache_log_categories` on the **cache** to send Azure Managed Redis connection logs to Log Analytics. Managed Redis has one log category, `ConnectionEvents`.

{ "azure_cache_log_categories": ["ConnectionEvents"] }

The destination works the same way as database log exports: the template's workspace when there is one, otherwise the workspace in `azure_cache_log_workspace_id`. The plan fails if neither exists.

Each flush sends a batch POST:

POST /api/jobs/{job_id}/logs
Content-Type: application/json

{
 "log_chunk": "Creating VPC...\nVPC created: vpc-12345\n",
 "stream_type": "STDOUT"
}

The Alethia API inserts the chunk into `job_logs` (via the `insert_job_log` RPC):

INSERT INTO job_logs (job_id, log_chunk, stream_type, traceparent)
VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4);

The `insert_job_log` RPC fires a Postgres `NOTIFY` on the `job_logs` channel carrying only
`{jobId, logId}` (an 8 KB payload cap rules out the chunk itself). The console relays it to the
browser over an SSE stream scoped to the `job_id` — the shared mechanism is described in
[Real-time updates](/concepts/realtime). On each notification the stream route fetches the rows newer
than the last id it sent and writes each one as an SSE message whose `id:` is the log row id. The
browser consumes the stream with an `EventSource` (`hooks/use-job-log-stream.ts`):

const events = new EventSource(`/api/stream/jobs/${jobId}`);

events.onmessage = (event) => {
 const row = JSON.parse(event.data); // { id, log_chunk, stream_type, created_at }
 appendLog(row);
};

A dropped connection reconnects on its own and resumes from the last row through `Last-Event-ID`,
so no chunk is lost or shown twice.

This module needs no cloud and costs nothing, so you can test attach, scan, plan, deploy and destroy.
Put it in a directory such as `envs/dev/`:

terraform {
 required_providers {
 random = { source = "hashicorp/random" } # on the default allowlist
 }
 # No backend block: Alethia sets its own state backend.
}

variable "greeting" {
 type = string
 default = "hello"
}

resource "random_pet" "name" {
 prefix = "${var.alethia_project}-${var.alethia_environment}"
}

output "pet" { value = random_pet.name.id }
output "greeting" { value = var.greeting }

